The webinar, led by speakers Ravi, Prakash, Ganti, and Devarajan, discusses the topic of set off in the context of the Corporate Insolvency Resolution Process (CIRP) and liquidation. The discussion is based on a judgment that addressed the incorrect adjustment of a refund by the Income Tax Department during the CIRP. The speakers explain the different types of set off, including statutory, equitable, contractual, and insolvency set off, and clarify their applicability during CIRP and liquidation. The common law set off, which is based on practice and custom, is also discussed. The speakers emphasize that the insolvency law aims to preserve assets, maximize value, and treat creditors fairly, and any set off must be explicitly mandated or legally justified to maintain the integrity of the distribution process. Contractual set off, recognized by the Supreme Court, is permitted if there is a provision for mutual set off in the contract prior to the commencement of the CIRP. Equitable set off, based on fairness, is allowed if it would be unfair to one party to not allow it. The speakers also discuss the applicability of contractual set off in insolvency, which requires an assessment by the resolution professional or liquidator. The discussion highlights the importance of understanding and applying set off in insolvency to create a balance for fairness in debt resolution.";}i:2;i:227;}i:12;a:3:{i:0;s:7:"p_close";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1626;}i:13;a:3:{i:0;s:6:"p_open";i:1;a:0:{}i:2;i:1626;}i:14;a:3:{i:0;s:5:"cdata";i:1;a:1:{i:0;s:436:"## How this particular seminar came about
